{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term (days 1–7): model is slightly high on average (~+0.1 to +0.4) and tends to underreact to sharp upside moves; apply a small downward bias unless a breakout regime is detected.",
    "Mid-horizon (days 8–20): systematic overestimation during pullbacks/mean-reversion phases (model stays too elevated while realized prices drift lower); damp forecast deviations toward the recent 30d mean by ~20–40%.",
    "Late horizon (days 21–30): regime-switch risk dominates—model often misses strong upside trends after a breakout (underpredicts by ~+1 to +8 in the Nov 18–22 samples). Add an upside trend kicker when the last 5–10 days show higher highs/higher lows (e.g., +0.5 to +1.0 per week, increasing with momentum).",
    "After spikes: model overshoots and remains too high for ~1–2 weeks when the market mean-reverts; cap post-spike forecasts using a decay factor (reduce excess above spot/short MA by ~30–50% over the next 10 trading days).",
    "Lag/level anchoring: forecasts appear anchored around ~68–70 even when the realized path trends strongly away; introduce a regime filter: if spot is >2 above 10d MA and rising, shift the whole path upward; if spot is <2 below 10d MA and falling, shift downward and increase mean reversion strength."
  ]
}
